d in the new phone's time zone; a file re-saved by a spreadsheet may lose that column's precision (falls back to Date + Time). Import is still Pro-gated like the other two, so a free user cannot move their history - a product decision left unchanged. |
 | 2026-10-20 | Search redesign (approved in the 2026-10-12 brief): the field is focused with the keyboard up on open. With no query and no filter the screen shows an idle state (`SearchIdleState`): the existing Needs-review banner, recent searches (on-device `RecentSearchStore`, newest first, capped at 8, de-duplicated ignoring case, each removable, clearable, wiped by Delete all data), quick-filter chips (Sent, Received, Today, This week, This month and the 3 biggest spend categories this month) and "Browse all transactions". Once a query or filter is active it shows a result count above the existing totals. Matching now also covers category (display label or enum name, at least 2 characters) and EXACT amount - `340`, `₹340`, `Rs. 1,500`, `1,50,000`, `99.50` - while malformed grouping (`1,2,3`, `12,34`) is just text. Text matching is trimmed. Rules live in `SearchLogic`. | Opening Search used to drop the user into every transaction ever captured, which is what Transactions/History are for. "Contains" on amounts would make 340 find 1340 and 3400, hence exact. `SearchLogic` was compiled and run with a real `kotlinc` - 61 checks - and mutation-checked (6 mutants: 5 caught; the 6th removed a redundant `^` that `matchEntire` already enforces, so it does not change behaviour). A recent search is saved on the keyboard Search action and when a result is opened; the keyboard-focus call is guarded because it throws if the field is not on screen (no transactions yet). **Unverified:** the Compose screen (no compile, no device). Recent searches are not in the backup file, by design. |
+| 2026-10-20 | PDF export (approved in the 2026-10-12 brief, rebuilt after the sandbox reset): a PDF button on the History screen exports the day, month or year being shown, built with the platform's `PdfDocument` (no library). A day or a month in the current or previous two calendar months is free; older periods and every full-year report need Pro (`PdfAccessPolicy`, `FREE_MONTHS = 3`; counted in calendar months, so on 1 November October, September and August are free). A free user sees a lock on the button for gated periods and gets the existing upgrade prompt, which replays the export after upgrading. An empty period shows "No transactions in ... to export" and never opens the file picker or the upgrade prompt. The file is saved through the system file picker and a snackbar offers "Open". Code is split into: pure data (`PdfModel`), pure paginating layout with a `TextMeasurer` (`PdfLayout`), a pure report builder (`PdfReportBuilder`), one money formatter (`PdfMoney`), and the thin Android `PdfReportWriter`. Content: summary, a month-by-month table for a year, spending by category, and a statement of every transaction, OLDEST first. Totals follow the app rules: needs-review rows count (marked `*`), UNKNOWN direction counts as neither spent nor received (listed, with a note). Text is plain: no emoji, `-` for minus, `...` for truncation, Indian digit grouping. | Table headers repeat on every page and a heading is never left alone at the bottom of a page; the gap above the heading is counted in the space reservation (the bug found in the earlier build's testing). The rupee sign is drawn only if the device font has it (`Paint.hasGlyph`), otherwise `Rs. `: that is one argument to `PdfMoney.format`. A pending export is stored as a string (`PdfPeriods`) so it survives the Activity being recreated while the file picker is open. The pure parts were compiled and run with a real `kotlinc` - 120 checks - and mutation-checked (12 mutants, all caught, including the heading-gap bug). The tests also found two real bugs before delivery: lakh/crore grouping was wrong, and a year table would have dropped a transaction dated after the phone's current month. **Unverified:** `PdfReportWriter` and the button/launcher wiring (no compile, no device); how the PDF looks; whether a real device font draws the rupee sign; the layout was tested with a fixed-width fake font, so real text widths may truncate a little differently. Very large years (thousands of rows) make a long PDF; not stress-tested on a device. |
 
